=base:main-content.cmark

# Roadmap

No package in this repository has had a stable release yet, and the
language itself is still settling — see the
[repository README](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj#roadmap-to-a-stable-release)
for where the language grammar stands. This page is about something
narrower: which *implementations* of tramaj exist, and which are wanted
next.

## Where things stand today

Two independent implementations target the same grammar and are checked
against a shared, language-neutral
[conformance corpus](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/tree/main/corpus) —
template/context/expected-output triples that both must reproduce
byte-for-byte, per case, in both `concrete` and `symbolic` evaluation modes.
That's a deliberately strong bar: a case belongs in the corpus only if two
independently written implementations can run it identically.

<table>
<tr><th>Implementation</th><th>Language</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj</code></td><td>PureScript</td><td>reference implementation, checked against the corpus</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-hs</code></td><td>Haskell</td><td>independent port, checked against the same corpus</td></tr>
</table>

Neither is published to a package registry yet (see the README's
"Publishing" section) — both are used today as workspace/source
dependencies. See [Integrating tramaj](/integrate-tramaj.html) for what's
actually usable from each right now, including the fold each one does or
doesn't ship.

## Desired: native implementations for JS, Python, and Rust

For a host in JavaScript, Python, or Rust, [Integrating tramaj](/integrate-tramaj.html)
currently recommends shelling out to `tramaj-cli` and writing a fold over
its JSON output — a real, usable path, but a subprocess boundary rather
than a native library. A native implementation in each of these languages
is desired, so that hosts in these ecosystems get the same thing PureScript
and Haskell hosts already have: an in-process parser and evaluator, with no
subprocess and no JSON round-trip required just to get a `Node`.

<table>
<tr><th>Language</th><th>Why it's wanted</th></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript / TypeScript</td><td>an installable npm package usable directly in a browser or Node host, in place of shelling out to <code>tramaj-cli</code> or compiling a PureScript wrapper</td></tr>
<tr><td>Python</td><td>native use from data pipelines, notebooks, and backend services already written in Python, without a Node/PureScript toolchain as a runtime dependency</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rust</td><td>a dependency-light, embeddable core for performance-sensitive or WASM-targeted hosts</td></tr>
</table>

None of these exist yet — this is a wishlist, not a schedule. There's no
committed timeline or assigned owner for any of the three.

## What "done" looks like for a new implementation

`tramaj-hs` is the template to follow, since it's the one implementation
that was added *after* the language had already settled on a normative
spec and output format. A new implementation is considered on par with the
existing two once it:

- implements the grammar and evaluation rules in
  [`reference.md`](/reference-language.html), including the static
  restrictions that make imports, action keys, and `ctx(...)` paths
  analyzable without evaluation (see [Properties](/properties.html));
- encodes and decodes the `Node` AST exactly per
  [`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html), including the round-trip
  guarantee (`decode(encode(n)) == n`);
- passes every case in the
  [conformance corpus](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/tree/main/corpus),
  in both `concrete` and `symbolic` mode, byte-for-byte against
  `expected.json`;
- optionally, but ideally: a fold analogous to `tramaj-halogen`'s
  `foldToHalogen` for at least one popular rendering target in that
  ecosystem (e.g. a DOM fold for JS, a common Python templating output for
  Python).

The v3 (`?`/`!`, [`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html)) and v4
(`%`, [`v4-types.md`](/reference-v4-types.html)) extensions are both
shipped and part of the same conformance bar — a new implementation isn't
considered complete while targeting only the v2 core.

## Contributing an implementation

If you're taking on one of these, the corpus is the fastest way to find out
what you got wrong: it's meant to be run against a from-scratch
implementation just as it's run against the two that exist, and a case
that byte-for-byte matches both `tramaj` and `tramaj-hs` today is a strong
signal your evaluation semantics agree with theirs. Open an issue on the
[repository](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj) before investing
heavily in one — coordinating on which language is being worked on avoids
duplicate effort.

